+++
title: Neo-Assyrian Empire
description: Assyrian history (911–609 BCE)
hook: By Karen Radner's estimate, a message could cross the 700 kilometers
  (430 miles) from the empire's western border province to the Assyrian
  heartland in less than five days. That speed was not surpassed in the Middle
  East until the Ottoman Empire introduced the telegraph in 1865, nearly 2,500
  years later.
standfirst: From the accession of Adad-nirari II in 911 BCE, and through much
  of the 9th to 7th centuries BCE, Assyria came to dominate the ancient Near
  East and parts of the South Caucasus, North Africa and the Eastern
  Mediterranean — the largest empire in history up to that point, and the
  fourth and penultimate stage of ancient Assyrian history.
key-facts:
  - name: Lasted
    value: 911 BCE to 609 BCE
  - name: Capitals, in order
    value: Assur (911–879 BCE), Nimrud (879–706 BCE), Dur-Sharrukin (706–705 BCE),
      Nineveh (705–612 BCE), Harran (612–609 BCE)
  - name: Biggest it ever got
    value: 1,400,000 km2 (540,000 sq mi), in 670 BCE
  - name: Ruled by
    value: A monarchy, from Adad-nirari II (911–891 BCE) to Ashur-uballit II
      (612–609 BCE)
  - name: Official languages
    value: Akkadian and Imperial Aramaic
  - name: Era
    value: The Iron Age
pull-quotes:
  - text: Sennacherib, the great king, the mighty king, [[ king of the Universe
      ]], king of Assyria, [[ king of the Four Corners ]] of the World;
      favorite of the great gods; the wise and crafty one; strong hero, first
      among all princes; the flame that consumes the insubmissive, who strikes
      the wicked with the thunderbolt.
    attribution: Excerpt from the royal titles of Sennacherib (r. 705–681 BCE)
    section: kingship-and-royal-ideology
  - text: I built a pillar over against the city gate and I flayed all the chiefs
      who had revolted and I covered the pillar with their skins. Some I
      impaled upon the pillar on stakes and others I bound to stakes round the
      pillar. I cut the limbs off the officers who had rebelled. Many captives
      I burned with fire and many I took as living captives. From some I cut
      off their noses, their ears, and their fingers, of many I put out their
      eyes. I made one pillar of the living and another of heads and I bound
      their heads to tree trunks round about the city. Their young men and
      maidens I consumed with fire. The rest of their warriors I consumed with
      thirst in the desert of the Euphrates.
    attribution: Inscription by Ashurnasirpal II (r. 883–859 BCE)
    section: reputation-of-brutality
  - text: mighty ruins in the midst of deserts, defying, by their very desolation
      and lack of definite form, the description of the traveller
    attribution: Austen Henry Layard, English archaeologist, writing in the 19th century
    section: archaeology
timeline:
  - when: 911 BCE
    what: Adad-nirari II accedes, and the Neo-Assyrian Empire begins.
    from: "accession-of-adad-nirari-ii: 911 BCE"
  - when: 879 BCE
    what: Ashurnasirpal II makes Nimrud the capital of the empire.
    from: "nimrud-made-capital: 879 BCE"
  - when: 853 BCE
    what: A coalition of western states assembles at Tell Qarqur in Syria against
      Assyrian expansion; Shalmaneser III engages it the same year.
    from: In 853 BCE, a coalition of western states assembled at [[ Tell Qarqur ]]
      in Syria against Assyrian expansion. The coalition included numerous
      kings of various peoples, including the earliest historically verifiable
      [[ Israelite | israelites ]] and Arab rulers, and was led by King [[
      Hadadezer ]] of [[ Aram-Damascus ]]. Shalmaneser engaged the coalition
      in the same year that it was formed.
  - when: 823–745 BCE
    what: The age of the magnates, when generals and officials rather than kings
      were the dominant political actors.
    from: "age-of-the-magnates: 823–745 BCE"
  - when: 15 June 763 BCE
    what: The Assyrian eclipse, which along with plague epidemics could have been
      read as the gods withdrawing their support for Ashur-dan III.
    from: These revolts were perhaps the result of the plague epidemics sweeping
      Assyria and the [[ Assyrian eclipse ]] of 15 June 763 BCE; both the
      epidemics and the eclipse could have been interpreted by the Assyrian
      populace as the gods withdrawing their divine support for Ashur-dan's
      rule.[[ ^65 ]]
  - when: 745–727 BCE
    what: The reign of Tiglath-Pileser III, who more than doubled the territory of
      the empire.
    from: "745-727-bce: Tiglath-Pileser III"
  - when: 729 BCE
    what: Tiglath-Pileser III captures Babylon and takes the titles "king of
      Babylon" and "king of Assyria."
    from: In 729 BCE, he captured Babylon, defeated Nabu-mukin-zēri, and thus
      assumed the titles "king of Babylon" and "king of Assyria."
  - when: 706 BCE
    what: Dur-Sharrukin, the city Sargon II built from scratch and named after
      himself, is inaugurated as the capital.
    from: In 707 BCE Sargon returned to Nimrud, and in 706 BCE Dur-Sharrukin was
      inaugurated as the empire's capital.
  - when: 705 BCE
    what: Nineveh is made capital, after Sargon II is killed in battle and
      Sennacherib abandons Dur-Sharrukin.
    from: "nineveh-made-capital: 705 BCE"
  - when: 689 BCE
    what: Sennacherib recaptures Babylon and destroys it, in an effort to
      eradicate Babylonian political identity.
    from: After a series of battles, Sennacherib finally recaptured Babylon in 689
      BCE. Mushezib-Marduk was captured and [[ Babylon was destroyed |
      siege-of-babylon ]][[ ^83 ]] in an effort to eradicate Babylonian
      political identity.[[ ^84 ]]
  - when: 671 BCE
    what: Esarhaddon conquers Egypt, bringing the empire to its greatest extent.
    from: "conquest-of-egypt: 671 BCE"
  - when: 626–609 BCE
    what: The Medo-Babylonian conquest of the Assyrian empire.
    from: "medo-babylonian-conquest-of-the-assyrian-empire: 626–609 BCE"
  - when: November 626 BCE
    what: Nabopolassar is formally invested as king of Babylon, restoring
      Babylonia as an independent kingdom.
    from: in the aftermath Nabopolassar was formally invested as king of Babylon
      in November 626 BCE, restoring Babylonia as an independent kingdom.[[
      ^112 ]]
  - when: 614 BCE
    what: The fall of Assur. The Medes capture the city; it is brutally plundered
      and its inhabitants massacred.
    from: "fall-of-assur: 614 BCE"
  - when: 612 BCE
    what: The fall of Nineveh, after a siege of two months. Sinsharishkun dies
      defending the city.
    from: "fall-of-nineveh: 612 BCE"
  - when: 609 BCE
    what: The fall of Harran, and with it the end of the dynasty and of Assyria as
      a state.
    from: "fall-of-harran: 609 BCE"
  - when: 605 BCE
    what: The Battle of Carchemish, a crushing defeat for the last Assyrian forces
      fighting alongside the Egyptians.
    from: The remnants of the Assyrian army continued to fight alongside the
      Egyptian forces against the Babylonians until a crushing defeat at the
      [[ Battle of Carchemish ]] in 605 BCE.[[ ^127 ]]
image-plan:
  - file: LammasuChicago.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: A giant lamassu from Sargon II's palace at Dur-Sharrukin. Several
      kings recorded in their inscriptions how hard it was to move the single
      massive blocks of stone these colossi were cut from.
  - file: Médio-assyrien.png
    role: inline
    section: background
    caption: The preceding Middle Assyrian Empire at its height in the 13th
      century BCE, approximately — the position the early Neo-Assyrian kings
      set out to recover.
  - file: Assyria_in_reign_of_Adad-nirari_II.jpg
    role: inline
    section: initial-reconquest
    caption: Early Neo-Assyrian borders and campaigns under Ashur-dan II (r.
      934–912 BCE), Adad-nirari II (r. 911–891 BCE) and Tukulti-Ninurta II (r.
      890–884 BCE).
  - file: Annals_Tukulti-Ninurta_II_Louvre_AO4655.jpg
    role: inline
    section: initial-reconquest
    caption: The annals of Tukulti-Ninurta II (r. 890–884 BCE), recounting one of
      his campaigns.
  - file: Alabaster_Stela_of_the_Asirian_King_Ashurnasirpal_II_(884-859_BC)_-_British_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: dominion-over-the-near-east
    caption: Stele of Ashurnasirpal II (r. 883–859 BCE), under whom Assyria rose
      to become the dominant political power in the Near East.
  - file: Shalmaneser_III_greets_Marduk-zakir-shumi,_detail,_front_panel,_Throne_Dais_of_Shalmaneser_III_at_the_Iraq_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: dominion-over-the-near-east
    caption: Shalmaneser III (right) shaking hands with the Babylonian king
      Marduk-zakir-shumi I (left). Shalmaneser was proud of the alliance, and
      had it made into art.
  - file: Stela_of_the_Assyrian_king_Shamshi-Adad_V_from_the_temple_of_Nabu_at_Nimrud,_Mesopotamia..JPG
    role: inline
    section: age-of-the-magnates
    caption: Stele of Shamshi-Adad V (r. 824–811 BCE), whose accession marked the
      beginning of the age of the magnates.
  - file: Stele_of_Bel-harran-beli-usur,_from_Tell_Abda,_8th_century_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum,_Istanbul,_Turkey.jpg
    role: inline
    section: age-of-the-magnates
    caption: Stele of Bel-harran-beli-usur, a palace herald, made in the reign of
      Shalmaneser IV (r. 783–773 BCE). He was one of the officials of the
      period who acted with privileges usually reserved to the king.
  - file: Tilglath_pileser_iii.jpg
    role: inline
    section: revitalization-and-rise
    caption: Partial relief of Tiglath-Pileser III (r. 745–727 BCE), who
      reasserted royal power against the magnates.
  - file: Capture_of_Damascus_by_Tiglath-Pileser_III.png
    role: inline
    section: revitalization-and-rise
    caption: A 20th-century illustration of Tiglath-Pileser III's capture of
      Damascus, taken in 732 BCE after three earlier Assyrian attempts had
      failed.
  - file: Tiglath-Pileser_map.png
    role: full
    section: revitalization-and-rise
    caption: The empire at the start (purple) and end (blue) of Tiglath-Pileser
      III's reign. By the time of his death in 727 BCE he had more than
      doubled its territory.
  - file: Sargon_II,_Iraq_Museum_in_Baghdad.jpg
    role: inline
    section: sargon-ii-and-sennacherib
    caption: Relief of Sargon II, founder of the Sargonid dynasty and in all
      likelihood a usurper who deposed his predecessor in a palace coup.
  - file: Reconstructed_Model_of_Palace_of_Sargon_at_Khosrabad_1905.jpg
    role: inline
    section: sargon-ii-and-sennacherib
    caption: A 20th-century reconstruction of Sargon II's palace at Dur-Sharrukin,
      the capital he built from scratch and did not get to enjoy for long.
  - file: C+B-Chariot-Fig6-SennacheribStateChariotNimrod.PNG
    role: inline
    section: sargon-ii-and-sennacherib
    caption: Line-drawing of a relief showing Sennacherib (r. 705–681 BCE) on
      campaign in a chariot.
  - file: Artist’s_impression_of_Assyrian_palaces_from_The_Monuments_of_Nineveh_by_Sir_Austen_Henry_Layard,_1853.jpg
    role: inline
    section: sargon-ii-and-sennacherib
    caption: A 19th-century reconstruction of Nineveh, which Sennacherib made his
      capital and then renovated between 699 and 695 BCE.
  - file: The_Destruction_of_Babylon_by_Sennacherib.png
    role: inline
    section: sargon-ii-and-sennacherib
    caption: A 20th-century illustration of Sennacherib's destruction of Babylon,
      an effort to eradicate Babylonian political identity.
  - file: Detail._Sam'al_stele_of_Esarhaddon,_671_BCE,_Pergamon_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: esarhaddon-and-ashurbanipal
    caption: Esarhaddon as depicted in his victory stele.
  - file: Capture_of_Memphis_by_the_Assyrians.png
    role: inline
    section: esarhaddon-and-ashurbanipal
    caption: A 20th-century illustration of the Assyrians capturing Memphis, the
      Egyptian capital, in the conquest that brought the empire to its
      greatest extent.
  - file: Ashurbanipal_in_a_chariot,_wall_relief,_7th_century_BC,_from_Nineveh,_the_British_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: esarhaddon-and-ashurbanipal
    caption: Ashurbanipal in a chariot, armed with a bow. He is often regarded to
      have been the last great king of Assyria.
  - file: Frederick_Arthur_Bridgman,_The_Diversion_of_an_Assyrian_King._Oil_on_canvas._Sotheby's.jpg
    role: drop
    section: esarhaddon-and-ashurbanipal
    caption: The Diversion of an Assyrian King (1876) by Frederick Arthur Bridgman
      — a 19th-century imagining that illustrates nothing the section is
      about.
  - file: Sin-sum2.png
    role: inline
    section: collapse-and-fall-of-the-empire
    caption: Impression of a seal possibly belonging to Sîn-šumu-līšir (r. 626
      BCE), the only eunuch ever to claim the Assyrian throne.
  - file: Fall_of_nineveh.jpg
    role: full
    section: collapse-and-fall-of-the-empire
    caption: Fall of Nineveh (1829) by John Martin. The city fell in 612 BCE after
      a siege lasting two months, and Sinsharishkun died defending it.
  - file: The_Battle_of_Carchemish.png
    role: inline
    section: collapse-and-fall-of-the-empire
    caption: A 20th-century illustration of the Battle of Carchemish, the crushing
      defeat of 605 BCE.
  - file: The_Fall_of_Nineveh.png
    role: drop
    section: reasons-for-the-fall-of-assyria
    caption: A second 20th-century illustration of the fall of Nineveh; the John
      Martin painting already carries the scene.
  - file: Nimrud_-_Neo-Assyrian_king.png
    role: inline
    section: kingship-and-royal-ideology
    caption: A Neo-Assyrian king on a relief from Nimrud. The king was not divine,
      but was seen as the divinely-appointed representative of Aššur on earth.
  - file: Seal_of_Queen_Hama_of_Assyria,_Nimrud_(transparent).png
    role: inline
    section: neo-assyrian-queens
    caption: The seal of Hama, queen of Shalmaneser IV.
  - file: 2018_Ashurbanipal_-_Tile.jpg
    role: inline
    section: elite-and-administration
    caption: A glazed tile from Nimrud showing a Neo-Assyrian king accompanied by
      attendants.
  - file: Servants_Dur_Sharrukin_Louvre_AO19878-9.jpg
    role: inline
    section: state-communications
    caption: Eunuchs carrying booty from a war, on a Neo-Assyrian relief.
  - file: Ancient_Assyria_Bas-Relief_of_Armed_Soldiers,_Palace_of_King_Sennacherib_(704-689_BC)_(a).jpg
    role: full
    section: military
    caption: Two Assyrian spearmen, from Sennacherib's palace at Nineveh. The
      army's equipment and organization are relatively well understood because
      the reliefs are this detailed.
  - file: Nineveh_-_Neo-Assyrian_soldiers_forming_a_phalanx.png
    role: inline
    section: military
    caption: Soldiers forming a phalanx, on a Neo-Assyrian relief.
  - file: Alabaster_Relief_Fragment_of_Tribute-Bearer_from_Palace_at_Nimrud,_883-859_BC.jpg
    role: inline
    section: social-classes-hierarchy-and-economy
    caption: A tribute-bearer, on a Neo-Assyrian relief from Nimrud.
  - file: Nimrud_-_captive_women_drawn_in_an_ox_cart.png
    role: inline
    section: resettlement-policy
    caption: A family of deportees leaving a captured Babylonian city in an
      ox-cart. Resettled people were allowed to bring their possessions and to
      live together with their families.
  - file: Babylonian_prisoners_under_Assyrian_guard,_reign_of_Ashurbanipal_668-630_BCE,_Nineveh,_ME_124788.jpg
    role: inline
    section: resettlement-policy
    caption: Babylonian prisoners under Assyrian guard, on a relief from the time
      of Ashurbanipal.
  - file: Library_of_Ashurbanipal_synonym_list_tablet.jpg
    role: inline
    section: akkadian
    caption: A Neo-Assyrian cuneiform tablet from the Library of Ashurbanipal,
      listing synonyms.
  - file: Nineveh_-_Neo-Assyrian_scribes_writing_down_the_numbers_of_enemies_slain.png
    role: inline
    section: akkadian
    caption: Neo-Assyrian scribes recording the number of enemies slain by soldiers.
  - file: Assyrian_Lion_weight.png
    role: inline
    section: aramaic
    caption: An Assyrian lion weight once belonging to Shalmaneser V (r. 727–722
      BCE), inscribed in Akkadian on the body and Aramaic on the base —
      evidence that Aramaic was officially recognized before Sargon II
      rejected it for royal correspondence.
  - file: The_famous_library_of_Ashurbanipal_at_Nineveh.png
    role: inline
    section: literature
    caption: A reconstruction of the Library of Ashurbanipal, which included more
      than 30,000 documents.
  - file: Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg
    role: full
    section: civic-technology
    caption: The gardens of Ashurbanipal in Nineveh on a relief (left), with a
      colour reconstruction (right). The right side shows the garden's
      sophisticated irrigation aqueducts.
  - file: Papyrus_narrating_the_story_of_the_wise_chancellor_Ahiqar._Aramaic_script._5th_century_BCE._From_Elephantine,_Egypt._Neues_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: literary-and-religious-traditions
    caption: An Egyptian papyrus from c. 500 BCE carrying the Story of Ahikar —
      the first attestation of a tale that went on to be translated into many
      languages.
  - file: Great_Semiramis,_Queen_of_Assyria_-_Cesare_Saccaggi.jpg
    role: inline
    section: literary-and-religious-traditions
    caption: Great Semiramis, Queen of Assyria by Cesare Saccaggi. Semiramis was a
      fictionalized version of the Assyrian queen Shammuramat.
  - file: Aswan,_Elephantine,_west_bank,_Egypt,_Oct_2004.jpg
    role: inline
    section: literary-and-religious-traditions
    caption: The isle of Elephantine in Egypt, seen from the west, where the
      Elephantine papyri and ostraca were found.
  - file: Peter_Paul_Rubens_082.jpg
    role: inline
    section: literary-and-religious-traditions
    caption: The Defeat of Sennacherib by Peter Paul Rubens. The Bible describes
      Sennacherib suffering a crushing defeat outside Jerusalem; the Assyrian
      inscriptions describe a resounding success.
  - file: Le_Tour_du_monde-04-p077.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: An 1861 illustration by Eugène Flandin of the excavation of the ruins
      of Dur-Sharrukin.
  - file: Khorsabad_Salle_II._Porte_H_-_attaque_ville_ennemie.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: An 1849 illustration by Eugène Flandin of a relief from
      Dur-Sharrukin, one of the drawings in Botta's report of that year.
  - file: Layard_Nineveh.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: An 1852 illustration by Austen Henry Layard of the excavations at
      Nineveh.
  - file: Hormuzd.Rassam.reclined.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: The Assyrian archaeologist Hormuzd Rassam, c. 1854. Layard's close
      assistant, he was appointed by the British Museum to carry on the
      excavations after Layard left Mesopotamia in 1851.
  - file: Translatio_imperii.png
    role: full
    section: ideological-influence
    caption: "The ideological translatio imperii: the supposed transfer of the
      right to universal rule from the Neo-Assyrian Empire onward, to rival
      early modern states claiming the same right."
  - file: Assyrian_soldier,_using_a_dagger,_about_to_behead_a_prisoner_from_the_city_of_Lachish._Detail_of_a_wall_relief_dating_back_to_the_reign_of_Sennacherib,_700-692_BCE._From_Nineveh,_Iraq,_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: reputation-of-brutality
    caption: An Assyrian soldier about to behead a prisoner from the city of
      Lachish, on a wall relief from the reign of Sennacherib.
  - file: Exhibition_I_am_Ashurbanipal_king_of_the_world,_king_of_Assyria,_British_Museum_(44156996760).jpg
    role: inline
    section: reputation-of-brutality
    caption: A relief of Ashurbanipal showing Elamite chiefs having their tongues
      removed and being flayed alive.
  - file: The_beheading_of_King_Teumman_of_Elam.jpg
    role: inline
    section: reputation-of-brutality
    caption: The beheading of the Elamite king Teumman, defeated in 653 BCE. His
      head was brought back to Nineveh and displayed for the public.
  - file: Assyrian_Relief_Attack_on_Enemy_Town_from_Kalhu_(Nimrud)_Central_Palace_reign_of_Tiglath-pileser_III_British_Museum_-_2.jpg
    role: inline
    section: reputation-of-brutality
    caption: The Assyrians besieging a town, on a relief from Tiglath-Pileser's
      palace in Nimrud.
glossary:
  - term: turtanu
    gloss: The commander-in-chief, the highest of the seven great offices; four of
      those offices, this one included, also carried the governorship of an
      important province.
    from: the //masennu// (treasurer), //nāgir ekalli// (palace herald), //[[ rab
      šāqê | rabshakeh ]]// (chief cupbearer), //rab ša-rēši// (chief
      officer/eunuch), //sartinnu// (chief judge), //sukkallu// (grand vizier)
      and //[[ turtanu ]]// (commander-in-chief). These offices were sometimes
      occupied by members of the royal family. Occupants of four of the
      offices—the //masennu//, //nāgir ekalli//, //rab šāqê// and
      //turtanu//—served as governors of important provinces and thus as
      controllers of local tax revenues and administration.
  - term: age of the magnates
    gloss: The period from Shamshi-Adad V's accession when prominent generals and
      officials, not the kings, were the dominant political actors.
    from: Shamshi-Adad's accession marked the beginning of a new age of
      Neo-Assyrian history, sometimes dubbed the "age of the magnates". This
      time was marked by the number of royal inscriptions being much smaller
      than in preceding and succeeding times, and Assyrian magnates—such as
      Dayyan-Assur and other prominent generals and officials—being the
      dominant political actors, with the kings wielding significantly less
      power and influence.[[ ^57 ]]
  - term: lamassu
    gloss: A protective stone colossus with the head of a human, wings and the
      body of a bull, set at Assyrian palaces.
    from: the great //[[ lamassu ]]// (protective stone colossi with the head of a
      human, wings and the body of a bull) for their palaces
  - term: issi ekalli
    gloss: '"Woman of the palace": the Assyrian title for a queen, sometimes
      abbreviated to sēgallu.'
    from: Queens were titled //issi ekalli// in the Assyrian variety of the [[
      Akkadian language ]], which could be abbreviated //sēgallu//, both terms
      meaning "woman of the palace".[[ ^147 ]]
  - term: kalliu
    gloss: The Assyrian-invented relay system in which each rider covered only one
      segment of a route, handing the letter on at a relay station to the next
      rider with a fresh pair of mules.
    from: The relay system, called //kalliu//, was invented by the Assyrians and
      allowed for significantly faster speeds in times of need, with each
      rider only covering a segment of the travel route, ending at a relay
      station at which the next rider, with a fresh pair of mules, was passed
      the letter.[[ ^29 ]]
  - term: hūl šarri
    gloss: "The \"king's road\": the empire's road system, which might originally
      have grown from routes used by the military on campaign."
    from: Called the //hūl šarri// ("king's road"), the roads might originally
      have grown from routes used by the military during campaigns and were
      continually expanded.
  - term: qēpu
    gloss: 'A "royal delegate": an official answering directly to the king, used
      to keep watch on institutions and officials across the empire.'
    from: The royal administration kept close watch of institutions and individual
      officials across the empire through a system of officials responsible
      directly to the king, called //qēpu// (usually translated as "royal
      delegates").
  - term: translatio imperii
    gloss: The idea that there is only one "true" empire at any given time and
      that the right to rule passes from one empire to the next, with Assyria
      typically counted first.
    from: The idea of //translatio imperii// supposes that there is only one
      "true" empire at any given time, and that imperial power and right to
      rule is inherited from one empire to the next, with Assyria typically
      seen as the first empire.[[ ^228 ]]
further:
  - slug: middle-assyrian-empire
    why: The empire (1365–1050 BCE) whose lost lands the early Neo-Assyrian kings
      spent decades trying to win back.
  - slug: post-imperial-assyria
    why: Assyrian culture endured after 609 BCE; this is what came next, and where
      the folklore about Sennacherib grew.
  - slug: hanging-gardens-of-babylon
    why: It is possible that the large park Sennacherib built near his Southwest
      Palace at Nineveh served as the inspiration for them.
anchors:
  hook: Per estimates by [[ Karen Radner ]], a message sent from the western
    border province [[ Quwê ]] to the Assyrian heartland, a distance of 700
    kilometers (430 miles) over a stretch of lands featuring many rivers
    without any bridges, could take less than five days to arrive. Such
    communication speed was unprecedented before the rise of the Neo-Assyrian
    Empire and was not surpassed in the Middle East until the [[ telegraph ]]
    was introduced by the [[ Ottoman Empire ]] in 1865, nearly 2,500 years
    later.[[ ^28 ]][[ ^29 ]]
  standfirst: The !!Neo-Assyrian Empire!![[ ^b ]] was the fourth and penultimate
    stage of ancient [[ Assyrian | assyria ]] history. Beginning with the
    accession of [[ Adad-nirari II ]] in 911 BCE,[[ ^21 ]][[ ^c ]] the
    Neo-Assyrian Empire grew to dominate the [[ ancient Near East ]] and parts
    of the [[ South Caucasus ]], [[ North Africa ]] and the [[ Eastern
    Mediterranean ]] throughout much of the 9th to 7th centuries BCE, becoming
    the [[ largest empire | list-of-largest-empires ]] in history up to that
    point.[[ ^23 ]][[ ^24 ]][[ ^25 ]]
